Take a small amount of the organic sample to be tested and place it in two test tubes:
(1) Drop saturated bromine water into the first test tube and shake. If the bromine water fades quickly (and no obvious gas is released), it means the sample may contain C=C;
(2) Drop the acidic KMnO4 solution into the second test tube and shake gently. If the purple color fades quickly and a brown precipitate forms or the color of the solution becomes significantly lighter, it also indicates that the sample contains C=C carbon-carbon double bonds.

First crush the aspirin tablets, shake with water to dissolve, and then take the clear solution:
(1) Take 2 mL of clear solution and add litmus solution dropwise. The litmus turns red to indicate that the solution is acidic; then add NaHCO3 solution dropwise to another clear solution. If bubbles (CO2) are generated, it indicates the presence of carboxyl –COOH;
(2) Take another 2 mL of clear liquid, add a few drops of dilute sulfuric acid and heat it to cause acid-catalyzed hydrolysis of the ester group. After cooling, add NaHCO3 solution dropwise. Bubbles continue to be generated, indicating that new carboxylic acid is generated; then FeCl3 solution is added and a purple or blue-violet color appears, indicating that there are aromatic hydroxyl groups in the hydrolyzate, and it is inferred that the original aspirin contains ester groups.

