exercises.stem:
Based on lab activity 3:
(1) Determine which of glucose, sucrose and starch are reducing sugars and explain the reasons;
(2) Write the chemical equation for the hydrolysis reaction of sucrose and starch under acidic conditions (can be expressed in words or a general formula).

exercises.answer:
(1) Glucose is a reducing sugar, because 10% glucose solution can generate silver mirrors from silver ammonia solution and reduce Cu(OH)₂ under heating conditions; sucrose and starch themselves are not reducing sugars. They do not undergo typical reduction reactions with Cu(OH)₂ when not hydrolyzed, but they generate reducing monosaccharides after hydrolysis under acidic conditions, making the solution appear reducing.
(2) Hydrolysis of sucrose: C12H22O11 + H2O → C6H12O6 (glucose) + C6H12O6 (fructose);
Hydrolysis of starch: (C6H10O5)n + nH2O → nC6H12O6.

